From 81af85a1004cacd6efe14a8eb0f5a890fd9d2026 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 06:35:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] drm/asahi: g17: Name the cause and the clients of every
 M5 GPU recovery

On M5 every firmware recovery logs "reason None slot_mask ... sampled 0
known_keys 0": the driver reads only the slot mask of the firmware's
recovery record, so a recovery never says why the firmware stopped or
whose work was running. With several GPU clients that leaves nothing
to act on.

The firmware fills the rest of that record in the primary status B
before it announces the restart (26C5035r primary TEXT 0x7808..0x7834,
the same code in 26B123):
- +0x4aac: the reason (bundle view 3 +0x48). 3 is a GPU page fault,
  5 a scheduling pause the hardware did not acknowledge within the HW
  data's pause timeout (macOS: "DSF Pause Timeout"), 6 a forced idle
  that timed out after a sleep request (27.3 on).
- +0x4aa0: the number of busy slots the routine walked.
- +0x4aa8: word +0x44 of the hardware record of the slot the report
  names, or 0.
- +0x4ab0: a u64 the reason's raiser leaves (a page fault stores its
  latched MMU fault status).
With the report's host-request word set, the routine zeroes the whole
area instead, slot mask included.

Read the record with the slot mask and log it next to the snapshot,
with the reason's meaning, the macOS kext's restart reason for it and
the queue the firmware's restart event blames. After the handshake,
name the client (process name and tgid) and the retained command count
of every queue the recovery table lists and of the blamed queue.

The record changes no blame by default: attribution on M5 keeps its
unknown reason. The module parameter m5_recovery_reason_blame=1 hands
the firmware reason to attribution as on G17P, so a timeout can move
the firmware's blame to the VM whose render was running.

